.TH PANFAN 8 "August 2026" "panfan" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
panfan \- fan controller for Panasonic CF-SC laptops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B panfan
.I command
.RI [ argument ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B panfan
hands fan ownership to Linux through Panasonic ACPI methods and controls the
TFN1 cooling device from CPU package temperature. It accepts Panasonic systems
whose DMI product name is CFSC-2 and verifies the expected thermal interfaces
before changing fan ownership.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR run " [" config "]"
Run the controller with the built-in or specified policy.
.TP
.B status
Show fan ownership, cooling state, and CPU temperature.
.TP
.BR check " [" config "]"
Validate the hardware and effective policy.
.TP
.BR policy " [" config "]"
Print the effective policy without accessing hardware.
.TP
.B passive
Enter OS-controlled fan mode.
.TP
.B failsafe
Set maximum fan and restore firmware-active control.
.TP
.BR pre " " action
Run the system-sleep pre-hook.
.TP
.BR post " " action
Run the system-sleep post-hook.
.SH CONFIGURATION
If
.I /etc/panfan.conf
does not exist, the built-in policy is used. A configuration file may override
any of these keys:
.BR poll_interval_ms ,
.BR start_temp_c ,
.BR stop_temp_c ,
.BR emergency_temp_c ,
.BR min_state ,
.BR max_state ,
.BR step_up ,
and
.BR step_down .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/panfan.conf
Optional policy overrides.
.TP
.I /proc/acpi/call
Interface provided by the acpi_call kernel module.
.TP
.I /run/panfan
Runtime ownership and sleep markers.
.SH SAFETY
Do not run panfan alongside thermald or another controller writing TFN1.
Normal daemon shutdown restores firmware-active control at maximum fan.
.SH AUTHOR
Toby
